      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "1. To solve the problem, we need to determine how many times Terrell must lift the 15-pound weights to lift the same total weight as when he lifts the 20-pound weights 12 times.\n   \n2. First, we calculate the total weight Terrell lifts when he uses the 20-pound weights 12 times. The formula to calculate the total weight lifted is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total weight} = \\text{weight per weight} \\times \\text{number of weights} \\times \\text{number of repetitions}\n   \\]\n   Substituting the given values:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total weight} = 20 \\, \\text{lb} \\times 2 \\, \\text{weights} \\times 12 \\, \\text{repetitions}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total weight} = 20 \\times 2 \\times 12 = 480 \\, \\text{lb}\n   \\]\n\n3. Next, we need to find out how many times Terrell must lift the 15-pound weights to achieve a total weight of 480 lb. Let \\( x \\) be the number of repetitions with the 15-pound weights. The formula for the total weight lifted is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total weight} = 15 \\, \\text{lb} \\times 2 \\, \\text{weights} \\times x \\, \\text{repetitions}\n   \\]\n   We set this equal to the total weight calculated above:\n   \\[\n   15 \\times 2 \\times x = 480\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   30x = 480\n   \\]\n\n4. Solve for \\( x \\) by dividing both sides of the equation by 30:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{480}{30} = 16\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\nTerrell must lift the 15-pound weights \\(\\boxed{16}\\) times in order to lift the same total weight as when he lifts the 20-pound weights 12 times.",
