      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine how many times Terrell must lift the two 15-pound weights to achieve the same total weight as lifting the two 20-pound weights 12 times, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the total weight Terrell lifts when he lifts two 20-pound weights 12 times.\n2. Set up an equation to find out how many times he needs to lift the two 15-pound weights to achieve the same total weight.\n3. Solve the equation.\n\n**Step 1: Calculate the total weight lifted with two 20-pound weights 12 times.**\n\nThe weight of one 20-pound weight is 20 pounds. Therefore, the weight of two 20-pound weights is:\n\\[ 2 \\times 20 = 40 \\text{ pounds} \\]\n\nIf Terrell lifts the two 20-pound weights 12 times, the total weight lifted is:\n\\[ 40 \\times 12 = 480 \\text{ pounds} \\]\n\n**Step 2: Set up the equation for the total weight lifted with two 15-pound weights.**\n\nLet \\( x \\) be the number of times Terrell needs to lift the two 15-pound weights to achieve the same total weight of 480 pounds. The weight of one 15-pound weight is 15 pounds, so the weight of two 15-pound weights is:\n\\[ 2 \\times 15 = 30 \\text{ pounds} \\]\n\nThe total weight lifted is:\n\\[ 30 \\times x = 480 \\text{ pounds} \\]\n\n**Step 3: Solve the equation.**\n\nTo find \\( x \\), we solve the equation:\n\\[ 30x = 480 \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{480}{30} \\]\n\\[ x = 16 \\]\n\nTherefore, Terrell must lift the two 15-pound weights \\(\\boxed{16}\\) times to lift the same total weight as lifting the two 20-pound weights 12 times.",
